      {
        "id": 158282,
        "name": "Pavane",
        "username": "Pavane",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/pavane/{size}/2406_2.png",
        "created_at": "2016-01-31T20:03:00.000Z",
        "cooked": "<aside class=\"quote group-playtesters\" data-username=\"Powerhound_2000\">\n<div class=\"title\">\n<div class=\"quote-controls\"></div>\n<img loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"\" width=\"24\" height=\"24\" src=\"https://forums.greaterthangames.com/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/powerhound_2000/48/1859_2.png\" class=\"avatar\"> Powerhound_2000:</div>\n<blockquote>\n<p>I don't know what hands you got for the heroes but a priority for me would be Infrared Eyepiece on Wraith so you can manage what is coming up. &nbsp; Stunning Bolt is also effective to use as only Plague Rats end of turn damage is irreducible. &nbsp; A great card to have in hand after Plague Rat has flipped is Fueled Freeze from AZ.A general&nbsp;tip I would have in this game is to take Plague Locus as soon as possible. &nbsp;A three hero team just can't take advantage as well of the bonus infected damage. &nbsp;Of his Ongoings Afflicted Frenzy is the one to get rid of when you can.&nbsp;</p>\n</blockquote>\n</aside>\n<p>I just won my first game. &nbsp;I have to be careful not to make bone-headed plays like Imbued Fire with Absolute Zero on the team, or cold-based self-healing for Absolute Zero when Cramped Quarters Combat is in play. &nbsp;I also realise that Drawn to the Flame is often Ra's best attack given the number of villain cards usually in play. &nbsp;A great combo is Fuelled Freeze to destroy three infections or other ongoing cards after Wraith has hit the Plague Rate with a Throat Jab to prevent any response attacks from the elimination of the infections.</p>\n<p>Thanks for your comments. &nbsp;I do try to kill the Plague Locus first and Afflicted Frenzies when possible, but I did not use Infrared Eyepiece. &nbsp;This is one of the most challenging random games that I have encountered. &nbsp;Replaying lost games can lead to insights that might take longer to discover if you just move on to the next game.</p>",
